# Worked Example: Wildcard Event Monitoring & Interaction Analytics

`aiBus` already emits a comprehensive, semantic vocabulary of every
meaningful UI interaction — a modal opening, a form submitting, a
command-palette item selected. Forwarding that to an analytics or
telemetry pipeline isn't a new feature to build; it's composition over a
mechanism that already exists.

## The recommended path: `useInteractionAnalytics`

For the common case — report real end-user interactions to your own
analytics destination — reach for `useInteractionAnalytics` rather than
hand-rolling the wildcard subscription below. It solves the two caveats
this file used to require you to handle by hand: several event payloads
carry real end-user data (`form:submitted.values`, `toast:shown.message`,
every `*:changed` event's own `value`) or a raw, non-serializable DOM node
(`element:resized`'s `target`), and a naive forwarder leaks the former and
crashes on the latter. By default it reports event type plus a small,
fixed allowlist of static identifier fields only (`id`, `formId`, `name`,
`componentName`) — never anything else, applied uniformly to every
channel:

```tsx
import { useInteractionAnalytics } from '#toolcrib';

function App() {
  useInteractionAnalytics(event => {
    // event: { type: 'modal:shown', detail: { id: 'confirm-delete' } }
    myAnalyticsSdk.track(event.type, event.detail);
  });
  // ...
}
```

`report`'s destination is always something *you* supply — toolcrib itself
is never in the loop, so there's no transmission-security question to
solve. Mount it once, typically near the app root.

Need a field the default allowlist doesn't cover? Pass a custom `sanitize`
— it replaces the default entirely, so call
`defaultInteractionAnalyticsSanitizer` yourself first if you want the safe
default plus a few more fields:

```tsx
import { useInteractionAnalytics, defaultInteractionAnalyticsSanitizer } from '#toolcrib';

useInteractionAnalytics(
  event => myAnalyticsSdk.track(event.type, event.detail),
  {
    sanitize: (type, payload) => ({
      ...defaultInteractionAnalyticsSanitizer(type, payload),
      ...(type === 'datatable:sorted' ? { direction: (payload as any).direction } : {}),
    }),
  }
);
```

## The underlying mechanism, for anything that isn't analytics: `aiBus.onAny(...)` / `useAnyAIEvent(...)`

`useInteractionAnalytics` is itself built on the same wildcard stream
every event already passes through — reach for `onAny`/`useAnyAIEvent`
directly for a use case that isn't reporting-shaped (a live debug console,
the demo app's own event log panel), where you want the raw, unsanitized
`{ type, detail }` rather than an allowlisted subset:

```tsx
import { aiBus } from '#toolcrib';

aiBus.onAny(({ type, detail }) => {
  console.log('bus event:', type, detail);
});
```

`type` is the event name (`'modal:shown'`, `'form:submitted'`, ...);
`detail` is that event's own payload, **unsanitized** — unlike
`useInteractionAnalytics`, nothing here strips real end-user data or the
occasional raw DOM node, so treat `onAny`/`useAnyAIEvent` as the low-level
primitive for cases that genuinely need the full payload, not the default
choice for analytics/telemetry. This is real internal plumbing, not a shim
built for this example — `openModal`, `showToast`, and every other helper
method still go through the same `emit()` path, so the wildcard subscriber
sees them all.

A few payloads carry a live DOM reference that a raw forwarder like this
has to handle itself — `element:resized`'s own shape is:

```ts
{{elementResizedPayload}}
```

Most analytics SDKs expect a plain JSON-serializable object and will
throw or silently drop a call carrying an `HTMLElement`. This is exactly
what `useInteractionAnalytics`'s default sanitizer already handles for
you above; doing it by hand over the raw `onAny` stream looks like:

```ts
aiBus.onAny(({ type, detail }) => {
  const { target, ...safeDetail } = detail as any;
  console.log(type, safeDetail);
});
```

## A sibling pattern already in place: `error:boundary`

The same wildcard mechanism, scoped to a single channel, is how
`<AIErrorBoundary>` reports a caught render error — see the `error:boundary`
entry in the Event Bus reference. Forwarding *that* one channel to a real
error-reporting service (Sentry, Bugsnag) is the identical shape of
composition as this file describes for analytics generally: subscribe,
forward, done. Reach for a single `aiBus.on('error:boundary', ...)`
subscription for that narrower case rather than filtering it out of a
wildcard stream built for everything else.
